---
title: Snippets
summary: Snippets cross an island boundary as regions. Static content freezes to server HTML, a snippet that has to wake hydrates on the far side, and region.snippet() is the manual form.
related:
  - regions/client-islands
  - regions/timing-nesting
---

A snippet is a function, and an island's props are serialized, so on its own a snippet could never reach the inside of an island. Yet `<Tabs>{@render children()}</Tabs>`, a forwarded `{#snippet header()}` and a wrapper that passes content into an island all work, because ogygia sends **every snippet given to an island across as a region**. This is the snippet form of the same primitive behind `<Component/>` regions, and you almost never call anything for it: children or a `{#snippet}` passed into an island work as they would anywhere else, including when a plain wrapper forwards them. The only time you reach for an API is when you build a snippet by hand from raw HTML, which is what `region.snippet()` is for.

## The two shapes of a crossing

A snippet crosses in one of two ways, and ogygia picks the way based on whether the snippet has to wake:

- **Static.** Plain content (prose, a code block, markup with no interactivity) renders **once on the server** and freezes to HTML. The client adopts that HTML in place, so nothing runs again and nothing ships. This is the common case for children.
- **Live.** A named `{#snippet}` whose body has to _wake_, because it renders an island or is interactive, compiles to its own entry and **wakes on the far side**. It renders inline on the server and hydrates on the client, so it is interactive where it lands, even after a boundary has forwarded it. Parameters are supported, and the arguments of each call ride across.

```svelte
<script>
  import Tabs from './Tabs.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
</script>

<Tabs>
  <p>Frozen prose — crosses static, ships no JS.</p>

  {#snippet toolbar()}
    <LiveButton />   <!-- crosses live: interactive inside the island -->
  {/snippet}
</Tabs>
```

Islands nested inside **static** content keep working. They render as full regions and wake on their own, even though the content around them is frozen.

A **live** snippet is part of the island that renders it, which has these consequences:

- An island inside it renders inline and hydrates with that island, the same as one placed in the island's own template.
- A server island (`render: 'deferred'`) inside it stays deferred. Its fallback ships with the page, its HTML arrives on its schedule, and its code never ships to the browser.
- It can render the host's template-root snippets, and read the host's `<script module>` imports and values.
- A lake (`wake: 'none'`) inside it stays frozen, and an island inside that lake wakes on its own schedule.
- It reads the page's contexts, on the server and in the browser.
- The island sets `data-hydrated` and fires `ogygia:hydrated` only after its live snippets have hydrated too.

## Forwarding through a wrapper

Composition is where this matters. A plain (non-island) component can forward its children into an island, and the crossing happens at the island's boundary, so the wrapper is ordinary Svelte with no special API:

```svelte title=CardShell.svelte
<!-- a plain wrapper -->
<script>
  import Inner from './Inner.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
  let { children } = $props();
</script>

<Inner>{@render children()}</Inner>
```

## Building one by hand: `region.snippet()`

When you need a snippet made from raw HTML, use `region.snippet()`, the counterpart of `region()` for components. It mirrors Svelte's `createRawSnippet`, except that the result is a _region_ snippet, which you can render in the same graph with `{@render}` and which can cross a boundary.

You can lift an existing snippet that takes no parameters, which freezes it as static content:

```svelte
{#snippet badge()}<span class="badge">stable</span>{/snippet}

<script>
  import { region } from 'ogygia';
  const frozen = region.snippet(badge);
</script>
```

Or you can build one from raw HTML, with the same `{ render, setup?, captures? }` contract as `createRawSnippet`:

```js
import { region } from 'ogygia';

const stamp = region.snippet({
  render: (label) => `<span class="stamp">${label}</span>`,
  setup: (el) => {
    // client-only wiring; dropped when the snippet is reduced across a wire
  },
  captures: ['shipped']   // serializable inputs, same law as island props
});
```

`render` must return one root element, which is the `createRawSnippet` rule, and `captures` must be serializable, the same as island props. `setup` runs on the client after mount. When the snippet crosses a boundary, `setup` is dropped, because a frozen snippet has nothing to start.
